            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Michael Kellogg, the case concerns whether a lower\u2011court decision that refused to enforce an arbitration agreement\u2014because it required class\u2011action procedures for antitrust claims\u2014was erroneous under the Federal Arbitration Act and the Sherman Act."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Michael Kellogg",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0000-0000"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 3,
              "start": 3
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Michael Kellogg, the petitioners argue that the lower\u2011court holding is reversible error for three reasons: it has no basis in the FAA or the Sherman Act, it creates an unworkable threshold inquiry, and it is unnecessary for legitimate policy concerns."
      },
      {
        "attribution": "Respondents",
        "citations": [
          {
            "document_hash": "6c9ca02ab89619ee4d04422844bbf2a100a82dda982232d048d801cba5a7355a",
            "evidence_ids": [
              "ev-6c9ca02ab896-0001-0001"
            ],
            "pages": {
              "end": 20,
              "start": 20
            }
          }
        ],
        "text": "According to Respondents, the respondent (Italian Colors Restaurant) contends that multiple claimants in arbitration can share the costs of an expert report."
      }
    ],
